From 44e13d5523f94fb3f06946f751af5e13112d7959 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vincenzo Grimaldi <vince.ceccarelli@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 23:31:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] 0040 join the book instead of crossing it (5682 passed)

The overlay's round trip is two perp legs. This account pays 5.5 bps taker
and 2.0 bps maker, so every fill this book has ever placed cost 11.0 bps
where 4.0 was available. On the Bybit settlement clock, same venue, impact
applied, the engine's gated rule, 0% borrow, 4.11 years, 79 trades:

    charged 5.5 bps/leg   fees $8,780.94   +7.23 %/yr   54 of 79 losing
    charged 2.0 bps/leg   fees $3,193.07   +8.59 %/yr   42 of 79 losing

+1.36 %/yr, and that is an UPPER BOUND: it asserts every entry rested and
filled, and nothing offline can establish a fill probability. The realised
value is fill_rate x that, first seen at the Phase D drill.

The spread is not the argument. Bybit's BTCUSDT touch is 0.1 USDT, 0.013
bps. The whole 7 bps is the fee tier.

WHAT RESTS
  Only the OVERLAY's entry. One order, and until it fills nothing has
  changed: the long side was the client's before this book existed, so
  nobody is naked while it sits in the queue.

WHAT NEVER RESTS
  The unwind, the margin-driven exit, the emergency sale of a naked spot
  leg, the delta rebalance, the buy-back of a short that ran past the
  inventory. Each REMOVES an exposure, and against the risk of not filling
  at all a cheaper fill is worth nothing.

  And both ACQUIRE entry legs. That mode holds a PAIR, and every second an
  entry leg rests is a second one side is unhedged. "Both legs land or
  neither" is the rule this engine exists to enforce; widening the window
  where neither is true to buy a fee tier would sell the rule for the
  discount. An AST test walks the engine and asserts patient=True appears
  in _open_overlay and nowhere else.

THE GATE STILL PRICES THE TAKER
  round_trip_bps() returns 11.0 under both styles. A gate that priced 4.0
  against a fill that came back taker is a trade admitted on a cost it did
  not pay. Maker is upside, never an assumption. --exec-fee-bps keeps the
  same separation offline: it changes what fills are charged, not what the
  gate prices.

THE DANGEROUS CASE
  A post-only order can fail in a way that leaves it RESTING. Crossing on
  top of that is two legs where one was intended. So no error code is
  interpreted: the venue is asked whether the order exists, on the
  deterministic orderLinkId. No row = rejected, cross instead. Resting =
  wait, cancel, cross the remainder. Query fails = the fate is unknown and
  the book HALTS. A cancel that arrives late is not an error either; it
  means the order filled, and the fill is read from the order, never from
  the cancel's response.

Leg now carries maker_qty/taker_qty: one leg can be both, and when either
half's fee is unknown the total is None, never the known half.

CARRY_EXECUTION_STYLE defaults to taker - exactly the behaviour of every
build before this one. CARRY_MAKER_WAIT_S is bounded to (0, 600].

Still not known, and written down: fill probability; the price of the wait
(~1 bps of zero-mean noise per entry at 2s and 40% vol, unmodelled);
whether Bybit returns a filled post-only from /v5/order/realtime (F10/F11).

